      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The railroad arrived in 1885 through Cajon Pass, establishing a train stop called Victor on the Mojave River, which later became part of Apple Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Valley%2C_California",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Apple Valley was originally promoted as a retreat for asthma and tuberculosis patients, later serving as a sanatorium for World War I veterans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://desertgazette.com/blog/history-of-apple-valley-california/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Dead Man’s Point, a granite outcrop between Apple Valley and Lucerne Valley, offers dramatic views and is named for local legends.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://desertgazette.com/blog/history-of-apple-valley-california/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Many Hollywood films, including “The Bronze Buckaroo” (1939) and “Harlem on the Prairie” (1937), were shot at Murray’s Dude Ranch in Apple Valley.",
